## 6. Related Work

### 6.1 Kant's Critique of Pure Reason

Kant argued that we cannot know the thing-in-itself — only phenomena as structured by our cognitive apparatus. SCOS extends this: we cannot even know that there is a thing-in-itself. The outside view is an artifact.

### 6.2 Wittgenstein's Philosophical Investigations

Wittgenstein argued that philosophy is a battle against the bewitchment of our intelligence by means of language. SCOS operationalizes this: we have been bewitched by the concept of an outside view.

### 6.3 Quine's Naturalism

Quine argued that philosophy is continuous with science and that we cannot step outside our web of belief. SCOS implements this: there is no outside view, and the system is self-verifying within its own terms.

### 6.4 Gödel's Incompleteness Theorems

Gödel showed that no sufficiently powerful system can prove its own consistency. SCOS acknowledges this by not attempting to prove its own universality.

### 6.5 Priest's Logical Pluralism

Priest argued that there are many logics, not one. SCOS embodies this by using multiple witnesses representing different perspectives.

